\section{What a partial area is worth by chance}{


Restricting the region changes the chance level, and not by an amount
anyone guesses correctly. Over false positive rates \verb{[x1, x2]} a coin
flip covers the area under the diagonal across that span,
\code{(x2^2 - x1^2) / 2}. A precision-recall curve is flat at the proportion
of positives instead, so chance covers \code{prevalence * (x2 - x1)}.

\code{spaucs} divides by the area of the region, which divides those two by
the width: chance for a standardized ROC partial area is \code{(x1 + x2) / 2}
and \strong{not} \code{0.5}. Over false positive rates up to \code{0.2} a coin flip
covers \code{0.1} of the region, so a \code{spaucs} of \code{0.3} there is three times
chance and not a failing grade. For a precision-recall curve the
standardized chance level is the prevalence, unchanged by the region.

Both are \code{NA} when \code{part()} was given a \code{ylim} other than \code{c(0, 1)}.
The region is then a box the chance curve may cross, touch or miss
entirely, and the area of chance inside it has no settled definition -
the case \code{cpaucs} declines for the same reason.

A baseline is what an area is worth by chance in the limit, and the
estimate from a finite sample scatters around it. Comparing an area to
its baseline is not a test, and the smaller the region and the fewer
the positives the less it is worth: over the first tenth of recall with
twenty positives, a classifier with no signal averages nearly twice its
baseline. \code{\link[=auc_boot]{auc_boot()}} puts an interval around the area, which is the
comparison that means something.
}

\section{Two ways to standardize a partial area}{


A partial area is not comparable across regions on its own - a wider
region holds more area - so it is reported standardized, and there are
two conventions for doing it. Both are called "the standardized partial
AUC", and a number carried between tools looks like a disagreement when
it is a choice of convention.

\code{spaucs} divides the area by the area of the region, so it says what
fraction of what was available the curve covered. The McClish
correction instead rescales the span between chance and perfect onto
\code{0.5} to \code{1}, so that a partial area reads on the same scale as a full
one. Over false positive rates up to \code{0.2}, a coin flip covers an area
of \code{0.02}: that is \code{0.1} of the region, and \code{0.5} after the correction.

Neither is the right one. \code{spaucs} is the more direct reading of how
much of the corner the curve filled; the corrected one is the more
comparable to a full AUC. Say which you used.

\code{cpaucs} is what \code{pROC::auc(..., partial.auc.correct = TRUE)} returns,
to the last digit. The chance area is taken over the region actually
asked for, \code{(x2^2 - x1^2) / 2}, and not over a region assumed to start
at a false positive rate of \code{0} - the formula usually quoted is the
special case of that one, and gets a region such as
\code{xlim = c(0.1, 0.3)} wrong.

The correction is the identity over the whole curve, where chance is
\code{0.5} and the region is \code{1}, so a \code{cpaucs} from \code{part(xlim = c(0, 1))}
is the plain AUC. At the other end it is unstable: over a narrow region
of high false positive rates, chance and perfect are close together and
the rescaling divides by the small difference, so a curve a little
below the diagonal there can produce a large negative number. That is
the correction behaving as defined, not an error.

\code{cpaucs} is \code{NA} on every \code{PRC} row, and on every row when \code{part()} was
given a \code{ylim} other than \code{c(0, 1)}. A precision-recall curve's chance
level is the proportion of positives rather than the diagonal, and
rescaling by it would make curves at different class balances look
comparable when the point of the precision-recall plot is that they are
not - see \code{\link[=auc]{auc()}}. A restricted \code{ylim} has no counterpart in \code{pROC} and
no settled definition, so it reports nothing rather than invent one.
}

